# Part of Cloudberry's isolation2_schedule (src/test/isolation2), as the port
# runs it on a coordinator and three segments: the tests of the schedule
# that bear on M3 -- distributed transactions, distributed snapshots, locks
# and the global deadlock detector -- and on M4, FTS and mirrors, in the
# schedule's order.
#
#   run   the test runs here, in both passes; cloudberry/ holds a difference
#         reviewed and kept, where one pass has one.  A third word names the
#         group it runs in, "main" when there is none: each group has a
#         cluster of its own, and the groups of a pass run side by side, each
#         in the schedule's order.  A group name ending in "*" is every group
#         that name begins, so gdd/prepare, which turns the global deadlock
#         detector on, and gdd/end, which turns it off, run first and last in
#         each group of GDD tests.  The groups are cut by time: each GDD test
#         waits out the detector's five-second period at least once.  The
#         group named standby has a standby coordinator too, and the group
#         whose name begins "mirrors" a mirror for each segment besides.
#   skip  it needs what the port does not have yet: the rest of the line says
#         what
#
# The schedule's other tests -- append-optimized tables, bitmap indexes,
# resource queues and groups, the rest of the fault injector's places in
# PostgreSQL's own code -- need M5 to M7, and are not listed.  Its FTS tests
# are M4's, and are: the ones that run are in the groups mirrors1 to
# mirrors3, whose clusters have a mirror for each segment -- three, so that
# they run side by side.
run  lockmodes                                     lockmodes
run  select_dropped_table
run  concurrent_partition_table_operations_should_not_deadlock
run  starve_case
run  create_index_deadlock
run  commit_transaction_block_checkpoint
run  prepare_limit                                 standby
run  gdd/prepare                                   gdd*
run  gdd/dist-deadlock-01                          gdd1
run  gdd/dist-deadlock-04                          gdd1
run  gdd/dist-deadlock-05                          gdd1
run  gdd/dist-deadlock-06                          gdd1
run  gdd/dist-deadlock-07                          gdd1
run  gdd/dist-deadlock-102                         gdd1
run  gdd/dist-deadlock-103                         gdd2
run  gdd/dist-deadlock-104                         gdd2
run  gdd/dist-deadlock-106                         gdd2
run  gdd/non-lock-105                              gdd3
run  gdd/dist-deadlock-upsert                      gdd1
run  gdd/delete-deadlock-root-leaf-concurrent-op   gdd2
run  gdd/update-deadlock-root-leaf-concurrent-op   gdd2
run  gdd/local-deadlock-03                         gdd2
run  gdd/end                                       gdd*
run  alter_blocks_for_update_and_viceversa
run  reader_waits_for_lock                         readers
run  vacuum_recently_dead_tuple_due_to_distributed_snapshot
run  distributedlog-bug
run  distributed_snapshot
run  segwalrep/die_commit_pending_replication      mirrors3
skip fts_errors                                    Cloudberry's DNS cache of segment addresses (get_dns_cached_address), which the port's dispatcher has none of
run  segwalrep/replication_keeps_crash             mirrors3
run  segwalrep/commit_blocking                     mirrors1
run  segwalrep/fts_unblock_primary                 mirrors1
skip segwalrep/recoverseg_from_file                gprecoverseg -i, which moves a mirror to another data directory, where the cluster file the port reads as a server starts says where a node is
run  segwalrep/mirror_promotion                    mirrors1
run  segwalrep/cancel_commit_pending_replication   mirrors3
run  segwalrep/twophase_tolerance_with_mirror_promotion  mirrors1
skip segwalrep/dtm_recovery_on_standby             the standby coordinator activated, which is M7's (gpactivatestandby)
skip segwalrep/commit_blocking_on_standby          a standby coordinator whose commits wait for it, as gpinitstandby makes one, which is M7's; and Cloudberry's WAL sender, which lets commits go on while a standby catches up, where PostgreSQL's holds them
skip segwalrep/dtx_recovery_wait_lsn               gp_stat_progress_dtx_recovery, Cloudberry's view of its recovery process reading the coordinator's WAL for committed distributed transactions, and a fault that fails the coordinator with a PANIC (exec_simple_query_start), neither of which the port has
skip segwalrep/select_throttle                     wait_for_replication_threshold, Cloudberry's throttling of a backend's WAL until the mirror has it, and gp_disable_tuple_hints, which PostgreSQL does not have
run  segwalrep/startup_rename_prepared_xlog        mirrors2
run  fts_manual_probe                              mirrors1
run  fts_session_reset                             mirrors2
run  sharedsnapshot
run  export_distributed_snapshot
